{"items":[{"index":11,"prompt":"Could you make a 1920×1200 map of a small working farm, seen from above? I’d like it to feel like a map someone could follow, with soft watercolor washes and loose contour lines. Show three cultivated plots arranged around a farmhouse and a curved farm track, with a pond near one edge.

Give each plot a clear outline and a different orientation: one long plot runs roughly east–west, one angles down toward the track, and a smaller one sits beside the pond. Fill all three with crop rows made from the same simple motif of gently curved, parallel lines. Repeat that motif by translating it into each plot, then rotate and scale it to fit the plot’s direction and size. Keep the rows inside their plot boundaries and make their spacing regular within each field, so the repeated pattern looks intentional rather than like unrelated marks.

Let the track connect the farmhouse to the fields without crossing the pond, and use a small bridge where it passes over a narrow irrigation channel. Add a few contour lines to suggest the farm’s gentle slope, but keep them distinct from the crop rows. A north arrow and a couple of modest map labels are welcome if they help. Keep the farm features readable; choose any other colors and details that suit the scene.","skills":["transformations"]}]}

{"items":[{"index":17,"prompt":"Prepare a technical diagram for maintenance trainees that explains how a hydraulic stamping press carries force through its frame. Show the machine in a straight-on front view, large enough that the structure and moving parts are easy to distinguish. Use a colored-pencil texture and a vivid palette, while keeping component colors consistent enough to support the explanation.

Make the upper crossbeam a clearly readable truss: divide the span between the two uprights into four equal rectangular bays, and split each bay with one diagonal, alternating the diagonal direction from bay to bay. This should leave exactly eight visible triangular openings. Keep the brace ends joined cleanly at the truss nodes, and leave the openings unobstructed. Beneath the beam, center a hydraulic cylinder and its descending ram over a broad press platen; show the platen above a sturdy worktable. Include a wide base joining the uprights so the load path can be followed down through the frame.

Use a few purposeful details—a pair of hoses, a control box, and simple guarding—to identify the machine without competing with the truss. Distinguish the ram and platen from the stationary frame by color and clear overlap, but do not cover structural joints. Favor an immediate visual hierarchy: first the press assembly, then its triangular bracing, then the supporting details. Keep the drawing diagrammatic rather than photorealistic, and omit decorative lettering or unrelated workshop scenery.","skills":["composition","relative_geometry","layering"]}]}

{"items":[{"index":22,"prompt":"We’re preparing the front panel for a boxed set of conversation cards used by libraries and community groups to help strangers feel welcome. The audience is broad—families, newcomers, and people meeting across generations—so the human figures should feel inviting and varied rather than like a uniform crowd. Please build the package illustration around a regular grid of human silhouettes, using repetition and controlled variation to make the pattern feel designed rather than mechanically copied.

Use exactly 30 separate figure cells, arranged in 6 columns and 5 rows. Each cell contains one complete, recognizable human silhouette; keep every figure within its cell and leave a visible gap between neighboring figures. Let five pose families—standing, walking, seated, reaching, and greeting—cycle across each row. Alternate mirrored versions in adjacent columns, and vary body proportions and clothing outlines across rows so repeated poses don’t read as duplicates. Keep the figures upright and legible at package scale, with simple, joined shapes rather than internal facial detail. The grid should remain evident even if you choose to suggest its divisions without drawing cell borders.

Treat this as real front-of-box packaging, not a detached pattern study. Make room for the product name and a brief supporting line, but let the silhouette system carry the visual identity. Choose the palette, overall placement, and rendering style to suit the welcoming purpose." ,"skills":["procedural_geometry","counting","positioning"]}]}

{"items":[{"index":47,"prompt":"I’m commissioning a map-style illustration for a small local-history exhibit, so visitors can spot the old Riverside Reading Room from the footpath. Please draw it on a 1920×1200 canvas. I’d like the building to be the clear landmark, with just enough surrounding context—a curving approach path and a simple indication of the riverbank—to make its location understandable. You can decide how the map is framed and arranged; it should feel like a welcoming guide, not a construction plan.

The reading room’s entrance is what I most want people to notice: give its arched doorway, shallow stone steps, lintel, and paired narrow windows distinct, recognizable shapes. Include the roofline and a modest hint of masonry so the facade feels like a real piece of architecture, but don’t let tiny ornament overwhelm its overall form. A small directional mark or brief place label could help if it fits naturally. Keep the setting quiet, without extra buildings or crowds competing with the landmark.

I’ll use this in a printed exhibit handout, so the route and entrance should remain easy to read at a glance. I care about graceful linework, considered contrast, and a few well-placed visual accents; I’m happy to leave the palette and drawing style to your judgment.","skills":["artistic_quality"]}]}

{"items":[{"index":49,"prompt":"We’re launching a compact basketball ball-handling kit for teens and adult recreational players, and I’d like its front-of-package artwork to make the contents feel connected through movement. On the 1920×1200 canvas, center a basketball at (960, 610), about 225 pixels in radius. Arrange exactly five satellite pieces of equipment around it: one training cone, one hand pump, one stopwatch, one whistle, and a pair of wristbands. Keep each silhouette distinct and fully visible, positioned at separated points along two looping paths; don’t let the gear overlap one another.

Build the paths as ellipses centered on the ball: an upright ellipse with radii 360 and 190 pixels, and a second ellipse with radii 650 and 130 pixels, rotated 30 degrees clockwise. Where the loops cross, weave them consistently: the outer loop passes in front at the upper-left and lower-right crossings and behind at the other two. The basketball should cover the portions of the smaller loop that fall inside its silhouette. Draw both paths behind the satellite equipment, breaking their strokes wherever a piece of gear would hide them. Keep all crossings and occlusions legible rather than tangling the contours.

This is a lively training product, not a technical diagram, so please render it with soft watercolor washes and loose contours while keeping the equipment recognizable. Include the product name “COURT CIRCLE” and a small “BALL-HANDLING KIT” line as part of the package design; let the ball and carefully woven orbit structure remain the visual focus.","skills":["layering","composition","constraint_following"]}]}
